{"source_passage_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0017-PASSAGE", "text": "Active Membrane Transport\n• Important carrier protein in human cells\n• Sodium-potassium pump (Na + /K + ATPase)\n• Most studied pump\n• Pumps 3 Na + out of cell and 2 K + back into cell\n• Present in all plasma membranes, but especially active in nerve cells and muscle cells", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "source_file": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf", "source_page_ids": ["HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0017"], "source_pages": [{"source_page_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0017", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "source_file": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf", "page_number": 17, "page_number_type": "slide_number", "slide_number": 17, "pdf_page": 17, "formatted": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf, slide 17 (PDF p. 17)"}], "slide_number": 17, "section_ids": ["HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0017", "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-PART03", "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "HHS3190M-COURSE"], "section_path": ["HHS3190M - Human Physiology and Functional Anatomy for Rehabilitation Services", "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "Active membrane transport", "Active Membrane Transport"], "content_type": "slide_text", "ocr_text": null, "status": "generated_not_verified", "verification_status": "generated_not_verified"}
{"source_passage_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0018-PASSAGE", "text": "Active Membrane Transport\n• Vesicular Transport (Bulk Transport)\n• Involves transport of many molecules, large molecules (E.g. large protein molecules), and fluids across membrane by forming vesicles\n• Requires cellular energy\n• Vesicular transport processes include:\n• Endocytosis: transport into cell\n• Exocytosis: transport out of cell", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "source_file": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf", "source_page_ids": ["HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0018"], "source_pages": [{"source_page_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0018", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "source_file": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf", "page_number": 18, "page_number_type": "slide_number", "slide_number": 18, "pdf_page": 18, "formatted": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf, slide 18 (PDF p. 18)"}], "slide_number": 18, "section_ids": ["HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0018", "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-PART03", "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "HHS3190M-COURSE"], "section_path": ["HHS3190M - Human Physiology and Functional Anatomy for Rehabilitation Services", "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "Active membrane transport", "Active Membrane Transport"], "content_type": "slide_text", "ocr_text": null, "status": "generated_not_verified", "verification_status": "generated_not_verified"}
{"source_passage_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0019-PASSAGE", "text": "Active Membrane Transport\n• Endocytosis\n• Involves formation of vesicles by invagination of cell membrane to transport certain substance into cytoplasm\n• Example: Phagocytosis\n• The cell engulfs a large particle by enclosing it within a vesicle called a phagosome. The phagosome is then combined with a lysosome.", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "source_file": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf", "source_page_ids": ["HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0019"], "source_pages": [{"source_page_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0019", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "source_file": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf", "page_number": 19, "page_number_type": "slide_number", "slide_number": 19, "pdf_page": 19, "formatted": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf, slide 19 (PDF p. 19)"}], "slide_number": 19, "section_ids": ["HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0019", "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-PART03", "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "HHS3190M-COURSE"], "section_path": ["HHS3190M - Human Physiology and Functional Anatomy for Rehabilitation Services", "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "Active membrane transport", "Active Membrane Transport"], "content_type": "slide_text", "ocr_text": null, "status": "generated_not_verified", "verification_status": "generated_not_verified"}
{"source_passage_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0020-PASSAGE", "text": "Active Membrane Transport\n• Exocytosis\n• Process where material is ejected from cell by secretory vesicle\n• Secretory vesicle contains the molecules. The vesicle fuses with cell membrane and release the molecules to outside of cell. ○ Examples ■ hormones, neurotransmitters, mucus, cellular wastes", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "source_file": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf", "source_page_ids": ["HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0020"], "source_pages": [{"source_page_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0020", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "source_file": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf", "page_number": 20, "page_number_type": "slide_number", "slide_number": 20, "pdf_page": 20, "formatted": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf, slide 20 (PDF p. 20)"}], "slide_number": 20, "section_ids": ["HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0020", "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-PART03", "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "HHS3190M-COURSE"], "section_path": ["HHS3190M - Human Physiology and Functional Anatomy for Rehabilitation Services", "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "Active membrane transport", "Active Membrane Transport"], "content_type": "slide_text", "ocr_text": null, "status": "generated_not_verified", "verification_status": "generated_not_verified"}
{"source_passage_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0021-PASSAGE", "text": "Membrane Transport and Membrane Potential\n• Selective permeability of cell membrane leads to different concentrations of charged particles in cytoplasm and in extracellular fluid. This results in membrane potential, the difference of voltage across the cell membrane.\n• In resting state, resting membrane potentials of all human cells range from - 50mV to -100mV, depending on cell type.\n• negative sign (-) indicates inside of cell is more negative than outside of cell", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "source_file": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf", "source_page_ids": ["HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0021"], "source_pages": [{"source_page_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0021", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "source_file": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf", "page_number": 21, "page_number_type": "slide_number", "slide_number": 21, "pdf_page": 21, "formatted": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf, slide 21 (PDF p. 21)"}], "slide_number": 21, "section_ids": ["HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0021", "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-PART04", "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "HHS3190M-COURSE"], "section_path": ["HHS3190M - Human Physiology and Functional Anatomy for Rehabilitation Services", "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "Membrane transport and membrane potential", "Membrane Transport and Membrane Potential"], "content_type": "slide_text", "ocr_text": null, "status": "generated_not_verified", "verification_status": "generated_not_verified"}
{"source_passage_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0022-PASSAGE", "text": "Membrane Transport and Membrane Potential\n• Generation and Maintaining of Resting Membrane Potential\n• (1) Proteins with negative charge are too large and polar. They cannot pass across cell membrane and will be trapped inside cell.\n• (2) Cell membrane is somehow more permeable to K +. K + are transported out of cells by facilitated diffusion.\n• (3) Action of Na + /K + pump brings more ions with positive charge out of cell.\n• Change of membrane potential is very important for the function of muscular system and nervous system.", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "source_file": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf", "source_page_ids": ["HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0022"], "source_pages": [{"source_page_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0022", "document_id": "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "document_title": "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "source_file": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf", "page_number": 22, "page_number_type": "slide_number", "slide_number": 22, "pdf_page": 22, "formatted": "05 - HHS3190MJ Physiology L3 (Jul 2026).pdf, slide 22 (PDF p. 22)"}], "slide_number": 22, "section_ids": ["HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-P0022", "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07-PART04", "HHS3190M-L03-PHYSIOLOGY-2026-07", "HHS3190M-COURSE"], "section_path": ["HHS3190M - Human Physiology and Functional Anatomy for Rehabilitation Services", "Lecture 3 - Membrane Transport (July 2026)", "Membrane transport and membrane potential", "Membrane Transport and Membrane Potential"], "content_type": "slide_text", "ocr_text": null, "status": "generated_not_verified", "verification_status": "generated_not_verified"}
